17:48.560 --> 17:50.740
To understand the evolution of this system over time,

17:51.220 --> 17:54.580
the author derives what is known as a coupled PDE hierarchy,

17:55.060 --> 17:57.100
a system of partial differential equations.

17:57.100 --> 17:58.280
Okay, PDEs.

17:58.660 --> 18:01.800
These equations describe how multiple variables change

18:01.800 --> 18:03.980
with respect to both space and time simultaneously.

18:04.280 --> 18:06.140
Which is notoriously difficult math.

18:06.280 --> 18:08.460
Oh, the full equations are incredibly complex.

18:08.720 --> 18:08.800
Yeah.

18:08.980 --> 18:11.540
Perhaps impossibly so to solve completely.

18:12.060 --> 18:15.480
So the author focuses heavily on the most physically tractable

18:15.480 --> 18:17.080
and meaningful form of this system,

18:17.440 --> 18:20.880
which is called the overdamped limit or the dissipative limit.

18:21.520 --> 18:21.960
Overdamped.

18:22.280 --> 18:24.980
Okay, for anyone who has ever driven a car with a bad suspension,

18:25.160 --> 18:26.200
you know what damping is.

18:26.260 --> 18:26.440
Right.

18:26.440 --> 18:31.280
If your car is underdamped, you hit a bump and the car bounces up and down for a mile.

18:31.580 --> 18:35.660
If your car is perfectly damped, you hit a bump, the suspension compresses,

18:35.780 --> 18:39.200
and it smoothly returns to its resting position without bouncing.

18:39.620 --> 18:40.060
Exactly.

18:40.060 --> 18:44.040
But what does it mean for the entire universe to be overdamped?

18:44.040 --> 18:48.080
Is it like a shock absorber that is so incredibly stiff,

18:48.160 --> 18:50.980
it just slowly, painfully oozes back into place?

18:51.200 --> 18:52.820
That's a brilliant physical translation.

18:53.820 --> 18:58.480
In an overdamped system, the inertial terms, the tendency of an object to keep moving,

18:58.900 --> 19:00.460
simply because it already has momentum,

19:00.960 --> 19:04.560
they become negligible compared to the forces of diffusion and damping.

19:04.560 --> 19:06.580
So momentum basically doesn't matter.

19:06.900 --> 19:07.100
Right.

19:07.320 --> 19:11.900
Think about trying to throw a baseball while you are completely submerged in a swimming pool.

19:12.020 --> 19:12.400
Oh, yeah.

19:12.600 --> 19:14.320
Your arm has momentum, sure,

19:14.580 --> 19:18.780
but the viscosity and density of the water totally dominate the movement.

19:19.180 --> 19:20.240
You can't snap your wrists.

19:20.320 --> 19:21.540
You have to push through the resistance.

19:21.940 --> 19:24.840
Why is that specific limit necessary for this theory?

19:24.840 --> 19:28.380
Why not let the universe bounce around like an underdamped car?

19:28.760 --> 19:34.240
Because the paper is ultimately trying to explain macroscopic irreversible processes.

19:34.780 --> 19:35.260
Like what?

19:35.580 --> 19:41.980
Human cognition, the formation of memories, the evolution of a language, the collapse of an economy.

19:42.420 --> 19:44.660
These are profoundly irreversible events.

19:44.760 --> 19:45.240
Oh, I see.

19:45.360 --> 19:46.540
You don't unthink a thought.

19:47.040 --> 19:47.420
Exactly.

19:47.560 --> 19:48.860
You don't uncollapse a bank.

19:49.100 --> 19:49.400
Mm-hmm.

19:49.620 --> 19:54.580
Irreversible processes don't swing back and forth endlessly like a frictionless pendulum in a vacuum.

19:54.840 --> 19:58.740
They happen, they generate entropy, and the system settles into a new state.

19:59.180 --> 20:02.300
But the overdamped limit of the master system captures this perfectly.

20:02.780 --> 20:09.580
Okay, so let's look at the actual physical phenomena being described by these partial differential equations in this overdamped limit.

20:09.720 --> 20:09.980
Okay.

20:10.180 --> 20:14.980
The paper highlights four specific behaviors that are constantly happening everywhere in the fluid.

20:15.500 --> 20:16.740
First, there is advection.

20:17.320 --> 20:17.380
Right.

20:17.480 --> 20:22.220
And in fluid dynamics, advection is just the bulk flow moving a quantity from one place to another,

20:22.220 --> 20:25.000
like the river current carrying the silt downstream.

20:25.460 --> 20:27.580
Second, there is diffusive smoothing.

20:27.980 --> 20:32.500
If you drop a drop of food coloring in a glass of water, it starts as a concentrated dot,

20:32.820 --> 20:37.720
but diffusion slowly smooths it out until the whole glass is a pale color.

20:37.840 --> 20:38.120
Yes.

20:38.320 --> 20:38.520
Yeah.

20:39.000 --> 20:44.480
And the third behavior is viscous damping, which is exactly that underwater resistance we just talked about.

20:44.700 --> 20:48.340
The medium resists sudden violent changes in velocity.

20:48.340 --> 20:50.980
Okay, so advection, diffusion, damping.

20:51.380 --> 20:52.000
What's the fourth?

20:52.200 --> 20:56.920
The fourth most critical component is how the system handles the entropy field, S.

20:57.800 --> 21:01.440
The equations show that entropy is actively sourced by kinetic activity.

21:01.620 --> 21:03.860
Which means movement creates disorder.

21:04.160 --> 21:04.440
Yes.

